    },
    "atom::a01e8693a7f8797a6b42e997": {
      "atom_id": "atom::a01e8693a7f8797a6b42e997",
      "source_handle": "P0005",
      "field_path": [
        "study_summary_A",
        "problem_or_question"
      ],
      "value": "The review aims to address the complex relationship between the microbiome and immune checkpoint mechanisms in cancer, specifically seeking to understand how microbiome composition impacts immune checkpoint expression/function, the challenges limiting anti-tumoral efficacy, and the innovative prospects for future anti-cancer strategies integrating microbiome science with immunotherapy.",
      "role": "evidence",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::2e9439305eb09bc0d83eda6d608462d7bc0a45f20585d0ab5806bbe1a51938b8",
          "field_path": [
            "study_summary_A",
            "problem_or_question"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::91c3ac87203b3f7382dce93290eb3eaa4c0db97e2441c93f7900da1a1f60834d",
      "multiple_values_in_field": false
    },
    "atom::1b4d9c3cfeb80380f15a8c54": {
      "atom_id": "atom::1b4d9c3cfeb80380f15a8c54",
      "source_handle": "P0005",
      "field_path": [
        "study_summary_A",
        "research_scope"
      ],
      "value": "The review covers the interaction between the human microbiome (primarily gut, but also lung, skin, and tumor) and immune checkpoint mechanisms (PD-1, PD-L1, CTLA-4, LAG-3, TIM-3) in the context of cancer progression and immunotherapy. It spans from basic molecular mechanisms to clinical implications, including dietary and environmental influences.",
      "role": "evidence",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::2e9439305eb09bc0d83eda6d608462d7bc0a45f20585d0ab5806bbe1a51938b8",
          "field_path": [
            "study_summary_A",
            "research_scope"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::0b6d869448517add0dd4f9e8b4a54db24ad5378a79b6093454489e9ef9a4e4fc",
      "multiple_values_in_field": false
    },
    "atom::93df419f23881e7bb626802e": {
      "atom_id": "atom::93df419f23881e7bb626802e",
      "source_handle": "P0005",
      "field_path": [
        "study_summary_A",
        "work_summary"
      ],
      "value": "This structured narrative review synthesizes current knowledge on how the microbiome modulates immune responses and affects the efficacy of immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs) in cancer. The authors outline the biological basis of immune checkpoints, discuss specific microbial species and metabolites that influence T-cell activity, and explore challenges such as inter-individual variability and dysbiosis. The paper highlights the potential of combining microbiome modulation (via probiotics, diet, or FMT) with ICI therapies to overcome resistance and improve patient outcomes, while noting gaps in understanding molecular signaling pathways and the role of non-gut microbiomes.",
      "role": "evidence",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::2e9439305eb09bc0d83eda6d608462d7bc0a45f20585d0ab5806bbe1a51938b8",
          "field_path": [
            "study_summary_A",
            "work_summary"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::f1b3d4d1a1a44234fc605f051dbb99b40eed926eac5133daf5e1e1c6f546e0d2",
      "multiple_values_in_field": false
    },
    "atom::5181e183b2c10026caf50473": {
      "atom_id": "atom::5181e183b2c10026caf50473",
      "source_handle": "P0005",
      "field_path": [
        "review_planning_B",
        "planning_summary"
      ],
      "value": "This review serves as a high-level conceptual map for linking gut microbiome composition to ICI efficacy in solid tumors. It provides specific examples of beneficial bacteria (Akkermansia, Bifidobacterium), mechanisms of action (SCFA production, T-cell modulation), and host factors (HLA, genetics) that may mediate this effect. It is useful for establishing the background of microbiome-immune interactions and identifying key molecular targets (PD-1, LAG-3) where microbial influence is hypothesized, though it lacks detailed quantitative data on specific tumor types or patient cohorts.",
      "role": "planner_interpretation",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::2e9439305eb09bc0d83eda6d608462d7bc0a45f20585d0ab5806bbe1a51938b8",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "planning_summary"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::89388be4b6f9b692d64e9a30c94b630ee10749b59ca6d05ebf610d5de5388d98",
      "multiple_values_in_field": false
    },
    "atom::bafd57110247206857a0e5b8": {
      "atom_id": "atom::bafd57110247206857a0e5b8",
      "source_handle": "P0005",
      "field_path": [
        "review_planning_B",
        "facet_contributions"
      ],
      "value": {
        "boundaries": "The review summarizes associations and potential mechanisms but does not provide specific clinical trial data or hazard ratios for these bacteria in solid tumors. It acknowledges that the exact molecular pathways are understudied.",
        "contribution": "Identifies specific microbial taxa (Akkermansia, Bifidobacterium, Faecalibacterium) associated with improved ICI efficacy and explains their general role in promoting anti-tumor immunity through T-cell modulation and SCFA production.",
        "facet_id": "F1"
      },
      "role": "evidence",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::2e9439305eb09bc0d83eda6d608462d7bc0a45f20585d0ab5806bbe1a51938b8",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "facet_contributions",
            "0"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::df37c62a237c74c9534b80db58c5878b61dbc347b80c7e184bdf12327fc77b99",
      "multiple_values_in_field": true
    },
    "atom::5abf22e97981accacfa8b730": {
      "atom_id": "atom::5abf22e97981accacfa8b730",
      "source_handle": "P0005",
      "field_path": [
        "review_planning_B",
        "facet_contributions",
        "possible_uses"
      ],
      "value": "Can be used to list candidate beneficial microbes when discussing which gut bacteria correlate with better ICI response. Helps frame the hypothesis that microbiome composition directly influences treatment outcome.",
      "role": "planning",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::2e9439305eb09bc0d83eda6d608462d7bc0a45f20585d0ab5806bbe1a51938b8",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "facet_contributions",
            "0",
            "possible_uses"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::a1801040df344207387418650c777f0ce8a27844eb106c10099a327be89ea3d5",
      "multiple_values_in_field": true
    },
    "atom::b6347179d994b6c43aa5ab53": {
      "atom_id": "atom::b6347179d994b6c43aa5ab53",
      "source_handle": "P0005",
      "field_path": [
        "review_planning_B",
        "facet_contributions"
      ],
      "value": {
        "boundaries": "The connection between specific microbial metabolites and these precise intracellular signaling cascades is stated as 'understudied' and 'unclear' in the text. The review links them conceptually but does not detail the direct causal chain for every bacterium-metabolite-target interaction.",
        "contribution": "Details the molecular mechanisms of immune checkpoints (PD-1/CTLA-4 signaling via SHP-2, ZAP-70, PI3K/AKT/mTOR) and how they lead to T-cell exhaustion. Also describes how dual blockade (PD-1/LAG-3) can reverse some of these effects by enhancing clonal diversity and IFN-γ production.",
        "facet_id": "F2"
      },
      "role": "evidence",
      "origins": [
        {
